    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Trenton was originally called Trent Town until 1784, when a General Assembly bill officially established the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.carolana.com/NC/Towns/Trenton_NC.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1784 the North Carolina Legislature created Trenton to serve as the new county seat of Jones County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.carolana.com/NC/Towns/Trenton_NC.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s courthouse, first a brick building burned in the Civil War, was rebuilt in 1939 as a WPA project.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.carolana.com/NC/Towns/Trenton_NC.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Until the 1920s, Trenton thrived as a riverport, shipping farm goods, lumber, and naval stores by steamboats along the Trent River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.carolana.com/NC/Towns/Trenton_NC.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A public library opened in Trenton in 1974, serving the community for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
